  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_bbea103f338f4bf99cb5eff6c3ccd8d2",
      "statement": "The Markowitz mean-variance input set has N(N+3)/2 parameters, of order O(N^2): 150 assets require 11,475 inputs and 350 assets exceed 60,000.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L16-L20",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2cae24b9b940b91392ebbf64a7acb28703bf92f5b8352c2386ad46d20fe32329:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_24baf1d736b74822834386d9b59d8576",
      "statement": "Correlations estimated pairwise need not form a jointly valid (positive-semidefinite) covariance: three assets with rho12=0.9, rho13=0.9, rho23=-0.9 give a correlation matrix with determinant -2.888, and weights w=(-1,1,1) produce a nonsensical negative portfolio variance of -0.1405.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L22-L30",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:401a3f281f0d6baac721d749007987056d0d55a5ffbd9e705a8a3f759825a6c2:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_4a04f626350f4d6bbe41c77ba21470ba",
      "statement": "In Sharpe's diagonal model the covariance between any two assets flows only through their factor loadings: Cov(Ri,Rj)=Bi*Bj*Q_{N+1}, so the N(N-1)/2 pairwise covariances need not be estimated directly.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L52-L56",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:d0f2bc3e48a528cd173517485564f9b701efe15570f434ea6120e909f80f6325:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_484be34823854c8cbfc66b1801e5a45b",
      "statement": "The diagonal model needs 3N+2 parameters versus Markowitz's (N^2+3N)/2 — under half already at N=10 (49.2%) and just 1.7% at N=350.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L58-L58",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:fd9fce57a368792f53be191f6233bf7b5da9e7230a329d41b86254167f5ded21:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_6d8d483f494a44d390e63d5ccc9f7f54",
      "statement": "The single-index model yields the compact covariance Sigma = sigma_m^2 * beta*beta' + diag(sigma_ei^2), requiring 3N+1 parameters of order O(N) — a rank-1 market term plus diagonal specific risk.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L93-L97",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:41edcaeec97d99f62378f23e255e95402d6293b795d47631867341b24af4508e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_b175d26bc3614e5ba40478c1398972f6",
      "statement": "In an equal-weighted portfolio under the single-index model the idiosyncratic variance is (1/N)*mean(sigma_e^2), which tends to 0 as N grows, leaving only the non-diversifiable systematic risk beta_p^2*sigma_m^2.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L99-L107",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:20a8bb9dab8da08d18c68596f478337adcce28922abe082354b75c8b5cafd182:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_9b5b5841fef844b5a8b8dbd07f150fcc",
      "statement": "Centering a factor (setting E[f]=0) changes the intercept but leaves beta_i unchanged because the covariance with the factor is translation-invariant; traded factors like r_m-r_f are often left uncentered so the return relation exposes the market risk premium directly.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L109-L119",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:cdc8fbf3ec79fceb9f1f3f9e4378906e6d4baaf97fdeef264367b3528460d100:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_aa5c9766f54e4408bff4f28fc1b14913",
      "statement": "The K-factor covariance decomposes as Sigma = B*Sigma_f*B' + Sigma_e with Sigma_e diagonal, and the systematic term B*Sigma_f*B' has rank at most K, so systematic risk lives in a K-dimensional subspace of R^N.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L129-L141",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:1379a4c815cfdabe893ee42ae474b02da6d9b3ef8469e71c5767aa9f261ed061:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3f5924b673a64149bc95361d9b8b2126",
      "statement": "Portfolio factor betas are weighted averages of the asset betas: beta_pk = sum_i w_i*beta_ik.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L143-L145",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:b378523df0489efd63f51e364413c994d88f94c0b3a3ae0e2bf4e2b0a355fd7e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_e94349a3a3174256896dcc447a271111",
      "statement": "For N=500 and K=5 the factor model needs about 3,500 parameters against 125,750 for the full covariance, cutting dimensionality from O(N^2) to about O(NK).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L147-L147",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:68c40455e9721d4227abd724450fffaddd4fc137cae156850c49b85f4463bf61:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3e1783f1141b4b7793ed7d01e4fb0368",
      "statement": "Under PCA of the S&P 500 excess-return covariance, the first principal component alone explains about 30% of total variance and the first five components about 50% cumulatively.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L157-L161",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:4737b27c6e2d7638fbfe4eb898e4dc20c69a8df15d4f8abecc842d5950cae4f1:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_fdbc013f2d8b49f0b28a741796dac885",
      "statement": "Under linearity, exogeneity, homoskedasticity and random sampling, the Gauss-Markov theorem makes the OLS beta estimator the Best Linear Unbiased Estimator (minimum variance among linear unbiased estimators).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L175-L181",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0c8c357784a68d92f73f538fec91a3033d63ed59094fe0dd718d1ab66859be77:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_94b4138714244afd876b22fa186a6e07",
      "statement": "The Frisch-Waugh-Lovell theorem shows that in a multifactor regression the estimated beta on one factor measures the relationship net of the other factors' effects.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L183-L183",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a575bb72a1f23e22fc19a9b6ab6c7d295c39cf9384ad1d864f468ed449f49f32:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_36e04296a1af45799a2cd81cf398ff44",
      "statement": "The Blume correction beta_{i,2}=0.67*beta_{i,1}+0.33 shrinks estimated betas toward the mean beta of 1, e.g. a raw beta of 0.743 becomes 0.828.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L185-L189",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:92262e57a12ebc6baca958e1be89fc3ed870456d37c6a294c678453117644c08:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_667fe7609b1b4ce6a6276e747861a6d8",
      "statement": "The CAPM's market-clearing tangency portfolio is the cap-weighted market portfolio, and the capital market line through it has the maximum Sharpe ratio, providing the theoretical basis for passive indexing.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L195-L201",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:ac662968ad94557081decf35afe9524cffcae8c404935be425577b408357b072:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_1c86b9da58d1461c9dcc839379792cca",
      "statement": "The fundamental CAPM equation E(rs)-rf = beta_s*[E(rm)-rf] with beta_s = sigma_sm/sigma_m^2 prices only systematic risk, not total volatility; graphically it is the security market line, which unlike the CML applies to every asset.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L211-L219",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:cb113fafa2f697302b8ad97e01fce3198fb41a1699b114001e72bb80cadf7115:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ba62e9cc093b41f4928fb8815cc96047",
      "statement": "The APT requires only a linear factor structure, no arbitrage, and enough assets to diversify idiosyncratic risk; imposing alpha_p=0 on well-diversified portfolios recovers the same SML as the CAPM but without its restrictive assumptions and without the true market portfolio.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L227-L241",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:d702e72f6b5376defc6a1702364efeb18bba952d60659209d76a8af34573edc1:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_4364196271184ce29e8d2427cb8be1b8",
      "statement": "Generalizing the APT to K factors yields the multifactor SML E(ri)=rf+sum_k beta_ki*lambda_k, where lambda_k are factor risk prices and a factor portfolio (unit exposure to one factor, zero to the rest) satisfies lambda_k = E(r_k excess).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L247-L251",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a6320ca4ea71d78bf0349175bdf74af60600118c87c641026dc401f8314f7698:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_20d36265be694335a7438e28d1043f91",
      "statement": "The first documented CAPM failure was the small-firm effect (small-cap portfolios earn more than their market beta predicts), and the Roll critique notes the unobservable market portfolio makes CAPM tests joint tests of the model and its proxy.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L263-L265",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a40c2f82d4471fb0dad04920ecc434d2feeefe1944309ca54497ac0c1a00a5c3:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_527ab81344b943469bc8e43ebb84982b",
      "statement": "The Fama-French three-factor model adds SMB and HML to capture the value premium the CAPM misses, reaching R^2 of 90-95% and readable as an APT implementation, while proliferating predictors produced the 'factor zoo' with data-mining and multiple-testing concerns.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L267-L273",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c0fdd56b942e9ba4268469adc322ce4ff1bb007a7ccff502cb5676c2e85dd40a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_2a9f327ebccb43098ba4201894169d7d",
      "statement": "On five assets with the three Fama-French factors, the full-Markowitz, single-index and three-factor tangency Sharpe ratios are nearly identical (35.30%, 34.46%, 34.73%), and accumulated estimation error in the full covariance can make it perform worse than the factor model.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L275-L277",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:b6a4d32739e36ae504deb431e7e3f7b3f99e8928a69f95001ce93caf6999c060:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_a2792c8c55c74fe8bec42667acd4649b",
      "statement": "Smart-beta strategies deliberately tilt portfolio betas beta_p=w'B toward priced factors, e.g. by maximizing beta_pk subject to 1'w=1 or a factor-structured mean-variance program with Sigma=B*Sigma_f*B'+Sigma_e.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L279-L283",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:98d99f6c4c14574220ddd3271c7365472364981671de3469598c7da25a3c4d3a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_8b9a90d71f694b95829f70bb7b555597",
      "statement": "Ridge regression (L2 penalty) shrinks all coefficients proportionally toward zero without zeroing them, while LASSO (L1 penalty) drives negligible coefficients exactly to zero, performing variable selection; the Blume beta correction is a special case of this shrinkage principle.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/05_factor_models.md#L293-L303",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:087942b6ee77d94f3764e400bb720d4693c12d1e6112814871cc8e70d9a3b8a0:"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
